WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:06.580
Na na na na na na na na na na na na na na
na na na na na na na na na na MATPAAAAAT!

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.560
Na na na na na na na na na na na na na na
na na na na na na na na na na MATPAAAAAT!

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:13.560
MATPAAAAAT!

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.080
MATPAAAAAAAAAAAT!

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:26.580
[Intro Plays]

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:33.960
Hello, Internet!

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.060
Welcome to Film Theory, one of the few series
that managed to make a successful crossover

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.320
from video games to the movies!

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.980
Suck on that, Prince of Persia!

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:43.980
[Air Horn]

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.140
But seriously, let’s get right into today’s theory because it’s one I’m pretty darn excited about.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.620
The new Suicide Squad movie is coming out and none of the trailers have shown Doomsday

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.040
yet, so overall, I’m fairly optimistic.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.100
But what the trailers HAVE shown is one of
my all-time favorite comic book villains:

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.100
The JOKER.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:04.110
Now, the thing I’ve always loved about the
guy is the mystery that just follows him everywhere.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.180
Who IS he?

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:09.140
It’s one of the longest-held questions about
the Batman universe, but recently, DC

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.000
has started to tip their hand a bit...at least in the comics.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.240
In Justice League #42, Batman gains access to the Mobius chair, an intergalactic chair

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.760
that has all the knowledge in the universe.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.320
After Batman sits in the chair, he tests it
by asking a question only he knows the answer to:

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.020
Who killed his parents? And the chair gets it right.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.820
So with the chair successfully proving that it knows all...or at least saw the opening minutes

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:35.520
of practically every Batman ever made, Batman asks the most important question of all.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.000
No, not "Who thought the Martha thing was a good idea in Batman V Superman?"

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.180
Or, “What WAS the deal with the Bat
Nipple Suit?” but the question that’s been

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.960
on everyone’s mind since the earliest days
of the comics: "Who is The Joker?"

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:52.280
We actually don't get to hear the Mobius Chair's answer, only Batman's stunned reaction.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:59.120
Then, 8 issues and a year later in Justice League #50 we finally learn what the Mobius Chair told Batman.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:03.729
Now, if you thought Game of Thrones has frustrating cliffhangers, then you’re gonna love this one:

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:09.120
In the issue, Batman reveals that the Mobius Chair told him there were not one,  not two,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.020
but THREE different Jokers.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.020
WHAT?!?!?

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.051
Way to milk it, DC.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.220
And of course, the internet lit up with theories.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.180
No Batman character was safe.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:19.860
Alfred? Clearly The Joker.

00:02:19.861 --> 00:02:21.461
Robin? Also The Joker.

00:02:21.461 --> 00:02:24.041
Batman himself? Somehow still The Joker.

00:02:24.041 --> 00:02:27.521
But your guess as to why is just as good as mine. So yeah, that's all well and good,

00:02:27.521 --> 00:02:30.941
but to me, the coolest part of this reveal wasn’t so much the implications for the

00:02:30.941 --> 00:02:34.401
comics, but rather the implications for the
movies.

00:02:34.401 --> 00:02:37.041
Because if there are three Jokers in the comics,

00:02:37.041 --> 00:02:40.941
could there also be three different Jokers in the DC film universe?

00:02:40.941 --> 00:02:43.361
Now that's not as ridiculous as it sounds.

00:02:43.361 --> 00:02:46.240
Just look to the man behind it all: Geoff
Johns.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.260
He’s a legendary comic writer and wrote
Justice League #42 and #50.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.640
The issues dealing with this Mobius Chair.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.300
He's the man responsible for DC Rebirth and recently it was announced that he

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.420
would be co-running DC Films.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:04.930
He’s literally the man whose job it is to translate the universe of the comics onto the screen.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:10.510
So if someone, ANYONE,  is going to adhere to this MONUMENTAL reveal from the comics,

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:11.390
it’s going to be him.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:16.280
And looking at the Batmans that are already in the movies, surprisingly, the theory holds.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.940
Up to this point, there have only been three
live-action interpretations of The Joker.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:25.830
In the 1966 Batman: The Movie, The Joker was played by Cesar Romero; then in the 1989 Tim

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:31.160
Burton Batman, The Joker was reinvented by Jack Nicholson; and, of course, in the 2008

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:35.300
Chris Nolan sequel The Dark Knight, Heath Ledger reinterpreted him yet again.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:39.550
Sure, there have been animated versions of the character as well, but in terms of just

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:41.350
live-action, it's a perfect fit.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:42.870
But those are the Jokers of the past!

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:47.290
We now have the inclusion of Suicide Squad, and it’s new Joker played by Jared Leto.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:51.330
That makes four different live action Jokers
- so what gives?

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:56.380
Well what if it turned out that Jared Leto
wasn’t a new Joker, but was instead picking

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:00.060
up the baton from one of the Jokers we’ve
already seen in the past.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:05.380
It’d be huge deal for the cinematic DC Universe,but it begs the question, which one would he be?

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.480
And is there even any proof that this assertion is even worth testing?

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:12.750
Well to find out for sure, we’ll need to
know a little more about the other three Jokers

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.880
and see how the Batman comic and movies collide.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:21.980
The history of comics can be divided into
four distinct periods: The Golden Age of 1938-1950,

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.680
The Silver Age of 1956-1970,

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.500
The Bronze Age of 1970-1985,

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:30.840
and The Modern Age of 1985-Today.

00:04:30.849 --> 00:04:35.169
And comics changed a whole bunch across those different ages - and along with them,

00:04:35.169 --> 00:04:36.770
portrayals of The Joker.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:40.830
The Golden Age was when the first super-hero comics like Superman, Batman,

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:42.550
and Wonder Woman were first being published.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:46.680
They became really popular right after the
Great Depression and during World War II,

00:04:46.689 --> 00:04:50.069
at a time when the U.S. really needed larger-than-life heroes,

00:04:50.069 --> 00:04:53.529
and as a result, feature patriotic heroes
doing battle against,

00:04:53.529 --> 00:04:56.800
uhh, let's just say "foreign" looking foes.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:02.360
It was during this age that The Joker made
his first appearance in the 1940 Batman #1.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:06.650
This first Joker was a deranged serial killer
with a sadistic sense of humor.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:11.990
In the first issue, Joker announces on the
radio his plans to steal a diamond and murder

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:16.129
a guy, then he just DOES IT. Just...goes and does it.

00:05:16.129 --> 00:05:21.249
In Batman's first twelve issues, he kills
dozens of people and does some pretty sick

00:05:21.249 --> 00:05:26.769
stuff, like burn smiles onto all his victims
faces and make masks out of peoples’ skin.

00:05:26.769 --> 00:05:28.560
Peoples’ SKIN.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.770
Is this the comics or Silence of the Lambs? Jeez.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:34.460
The Golden Age of Comics wasn't just superhero stories though.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:38.720
There were actually all kinds of comics: horror, crime, westerns, romance.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.660
And they weren’t just for kids either, a lot of them had themes were pretty darn adult.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:47.240
In the middle of the 1950s anti-communist
paranoia though, Fredric Wertham released

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:52.400
"The Seduction of the Innocent" where he blamed comic books for all "juvenile delinquency".

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.720
Hmm, sound familiar at all? So obviously, as people began to get more sensitive,

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.320
there became an increased call to tone down the violence in these books.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.440
However, instead of waiting for the government to censor them,

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.800
comic book publishers censored themselves.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.640
In 1954, The Comics Code Authority was formed to review and approve comics...by their own rules.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:15.100
Rules like: In comics, "Crimes shall never
be presented to create sympathy for the criminal",

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:19.400
"Romance stories shall emphasize the value
of the home and the sanctity of marriage.",

00:06:19.409 --> 00:06:24.659
and "All scenes of horror, bloodshed, gory
or gruesome crimes...shall not be permitted."

00:06:24.659 --> 00:06:25.999
as well as 37 other rules.

00:06:25.999 --> 00:06:28.759
With these changes began the Silver Age of
Comics.

00:06:28.759 --> 00:06:33.600
The Silver Age, often referred to as the "Age
of Innocence", was all about fantasy and optimism.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.909
Since crime and horror were heavily regulated,
superheroes started battling monsters and

00:06:37.909 --> 00:06:39.380
aliens instead of people.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:42.599
The grittiness of The Golden Age was gone
and replaced with campy humor.

00:06:42.599 --> 00:06:44.569
And the Joker was no exception.

00:06:44.569 --> 00:06:49.920
In the 1950s and 60’s, the Joker went from
being a mass murderer to being just a prankster.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.340
He was like a tamer version of Roman Atwood!

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:54.880
All the Joker wanted during the Silver Age
was cash.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.159
In fact, in a 1952 issue, "Joker's Millions",
The Joker inherits a massive fortune and just

00:07:00.159 --> 00:07:03.359
retires from crime altogether! Peace out, guys, I'm done!

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.219
Until of course it turns out his whole fortune is counterfeit.

00:07:06.219 --> 00:07:07.910
Ugh, I hate when that happens!

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:10.479
The Joker here never kills or even harms anyone.

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:13.259
It’s like Looney Tunes with Elmer Fudd and Bugs Bunny,

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.080
where he basically just gets out-pranked by Batman.

00:07:16.089 --> 00:07:20.749
This is also where he got all his gadgets:
hand buzzer, spitting flower, trick guns and

00:07:20.749 --> 00:07:22.629
even his own car: The Jokermobile.

00:07:22.629 --> 00:07:26.999
It took 20 years, but eventually people got
sick of the saccharine Comics Code Authority

00:07:26.999 --> 00:07:30.279
and basically stopped following it, ushering in The Bronze Age.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:34.960
Now, The Bronze Age of comics brought back comics
as a place to address social issues and return

00:07:34.969 --> 00:07:36.509
them to their darker roots.

00:07:36.509 --> 00:07:40.879
This is where Iron Man dealt with alcoholism,
Spiderman's girlfriend Gwen Stacey died and

00:07:40.879 --> 00:07:43.419
The Joker became a murderer again.

00:07:43.419 --> 00:07:44.419
YAY!

00:07:44.419 --> 00:07:47.930
The difference between this and the Golden
Age, though, is that now there was a big emphasis

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:49.729
on the psychology of these characters.

00:07:49.729 --> 00:07:54.539
For instance, in 1973's The Joker's Five-Way
Revenge, The Joker escapes from an insane

00:07:54.539 --> 00:07:58.189
asylum and systematically kills the five men
who ratted him out.

00:07:58.189 --> 00:08:02.789
The new version of the Joker emphasized his
insanity and mental instability.

00:08:02.789 --> 00:08:07.779
Instead of an evil master criminal or a goofy
prankster, The Joker is a mentally ill psychopath

00:08:07.779 --> 00:08:10.110
who chooses not to control his actions.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:13.550
The Modern Age of Comics doubled down on this dark and gritty imagery.

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:17.810
The best example is The Dark Knight Returns, which was a comic before it was a movie.

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:18.710
Surprise!

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:22.800
And just like to movie, the comic emphasized the fact that the Joker can’t function or

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:27.140
or practically even exist without Batman as his counterpart; the yin to his yang.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:28.940
All of this should be sounding familiar.

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:33.380
So we have three ages of comics and three
Jokers: the criminal mastermind; the goofy

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:35.130
prankster; and the psychopath.

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:38.959
When Geoff Johns announced there were three
different Jokers in Justice League #50, he

00:08:38.959 --> 00:08:43.099
was basically explaining away how the character has evolved over the past eighty years.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:47.220
it could be interpreted that the three Jokers of The Gold, Silver and Bronze/Modern
Age

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:49.920
have now just become their own separate characters.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:53.600
But if it’s true in the comics, does it also work in the movies?

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.860
Surprisingly, yes!

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:59.930
Because when you look at it, the three film
versions of The Joker seamlessly correspond

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:01.610
to the three comic versions.

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:08.220
The 1966 Cesar Romero Joker is the Silver
Age Joker: a goofy prankster with crazy schemes,

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:10.010
constantly outwitted by Batman.

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:15.360
Romero's Joker isn't a murderer or a sadistic
madman, he’s just a thief; obsessed with

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:16.970
robbing banks and art museums.

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:20.900
And much like The Silver Age Joker, Romero's version uses a variety of gadgets:

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:27.600
a spraying flower, a utility belt, hand buzzers, gas grenades and yes, even his own car: The Jokermobile.

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:32.470
Tim Burton's 1989 Jack Nicholson Joker is a completely different take,

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:35.120
which makes sense because he’s the Golden Age version.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:39.080
The Nicholson Joker's origin story follows
the exact same story that’s given to the Golden

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:43.220
Age Joker, where he’s a career criminal
who slips into a vat of chemicals after being

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:44.709
cornered by Batman.

00:09:44.709 --> 00:09:48.620
He’s disfigured and when he sees his reflection
at the surgeon's office, he goes completely

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:52.840
insane - reinventing himself as The Joker,
beat-for-beat like the comic.

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:57.460
Nicholson's Joker is also a serious killer,
contorting his victim's faces into permanent

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:01.720
smiles with toxins - the same calling card
as The Golden Age Joker.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:06.160
The Nicholson Joker announces his plans on
television - just as The Golden Age Joker

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:12.040
announced his plans on the radio in Batman
#1, and even uses the same flesh makeup technique.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:17.000
That leaves us with The Heath Ledger Joker,
the tragic psychopath.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:21.260
He tells Batman that he can't live without him, that they're destined to be adversaries forever.

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:23.420
"I don't want to kill you.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:27.500
What would I do without you? You complete me."

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:32.260
People are constantly stating how crazy Ledger's Joker is—how unpredictable he is,

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:36.260
which reflects The Bronze and Modern Age's focus on his unstable psyche.

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:39.620
"Some men just want to watch the world burn."

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:43.860
So there you have it, all three Jokers fit cleanly into the three Joker comic model.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:48.800
But then that prompts the question: Now that Jared Leto is playing the Joker in Suicide Squad, is

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:52.339
he a new character, or which Joker line will
he be continuing?

00:10:52.339 --> 00:10:54.630
Are we ACTUALLY going to be getting to Suicide
Squad now?

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:58.540
Mobius Chair, tell me: who is Jared Leto's
Joker?

00:10:58.540 --> 00:10:59.540
WHAT?

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:02.220
Are you sure? No—I'm not questioning you...

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.190
But what sort of proof do you have?...

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:06.850
Oh, that makes a lot of sense, actually...

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:07.959
That would make a really good episode...

00:11:07.959 --> 00:11:11.279
But then I’d have to do that thing where
there’s a cliffhanger and I HATE those in

00:11:11.279 --> 00:11:13.259
TV shows, it’s the WORST!

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:16.040
I haven’t even done Dr. Who part 3 yet, Mobius Chair!

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.580
[As a 1950's announcer]
What did The Mobius Chair tell MatPat?

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:21.370
Who really is Jared Leto's Joker?

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:24.330
Would they really end on a cliffhanger after just forcing you to wait

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:26.230
for part two of Don’t Hug Me I’m Scared?

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:28.330
Unbelievable!! Subscribe, so you can

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:30.640
join us next week on Film Theory to find out!

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:32.840
And, while you're in the mood for MORE Joker action,

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:35.740
make sure you click right here to check out the Wisecrack channel's

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:38.600
video on the philosophy of The Joker!

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.060
Revealing the character's original inspirations,

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:43.060
the true meaning behind his motives,

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:46.120
and how his evolution over the years reflects the current state of our times!

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:49.240
The folks over at Wisecrack, always delivering loads of education,

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:51.740
with loads of laughs, do check them out!

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:56.700
And DO make sure that you're here next week for the thrilling conclusion of this BAT SAGA!

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:59.180
And in the meantime, remember:

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:03.360
That's just a theory,
a FILM theory.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:05.340
Aaaaaaaand Cut.

